# Measurements that get regenerated by the checker that reads them.
_probe/width_report.json

# cargo build output (Rust crate)
target/

# 19 MB of measured offsets, regenerated in ~40 s by _probe/make_stat_offsets.py
_golden/stat_offsets.json
_golden/unit0_trace.txt

# 20.5 MB of per-unit cost/attack/armour offsets with ZERO readers.  Measured on
# 2026-10-28: no Rust file names it, no Python file opens it, and the only
# generator that writes it (`_probe/make_unit_slots.py`) is called by nothing.
# The Rust reader models those three lists itself -- `Unit.costs` and
# `Type50.attacks/armors` in `desktop/aoe2mod-dat/src/stage810.rs`, each entry
# carrying the offset it was parsed at -- so this table is not a stale copy of a
# live answer, it is an answer to a question no caller asks.  It stayed in git
# and in the stamp manifest only because nothing ever asked whether it had a
# reader; a 20 MB file that must be regenerated before the stamp can be written
# is a build dependency on a file that changes no behaviour.
_golden/unit_slots.json

# 81 KB per-unit spans, written by `_probe/make_unit_spans.py` so the Rust port
# could be diffed record by record against Python.  The port finished; the file
# has no reader (the `NU.unit_spans` hits in `aoe2mod/` are the FUNCTION, which
# walks the record stream at runtime, not this dump).  Regenerated in seconds
# when a future port needs the same ladder.
_golden/unit_spans.json

# Tauri's generated capability schemas.  Rewritten by `tauri-build` on every
# compile from the capabilities/ folder, so it is output that happens to sit
# in the source tree.  Committing it means every build produces a diff.
desktop/src-tauri/gen/

# Scratch .dat files written by the probe runs (hp1000.dat, test_hp.dat).
# They are copies of the game's own data with a few bytes changed -- 23 MB of
# it -- and any probe regenerates them in seconds.
_out/
_golden/sprite_art.json
_golden/sprite_pairs.json

# build output and cargo cache relocated into the repo
_cache/
_probe/_tmp/

# Signing keys for the sealed build (_probe/protect/seal.py).  priv.hex is the
# only thing standing between "a repacked folder is refused" and "a repacked
# folder is indistinguishable from ours", so it lives on one machine and is
# never a commit.  pub.hex and master.hex ride along because a half-published
# key set is worse than none: the master alone undoes the pack encryption.
_probe/protect/keys/

# The official .dat shelf (aoe2mod/mod/archives.py).  One blob per distinct
# record-format generation, 313 MB for the 53 generations the upgrade engine
# needs -- the game's own data, not this project's source.  It is what makes
# 「一键适配」 possible at all, so it must NOT be deleted; it must also not be
# committed: a clone would carry the shipped game data.  It refills itself
# the first time the tool opens with the game installed.
dat_archive/

# The operation record.  `aoe2mod/oplog.py` appends one line per edit to `log/`
# beside the tool, which for a source checkout is this repository.  It describes
# what one person did on one machine -- the same reason `_probe/out_*.log` is
# ignored -- and a committed copy would be a diff after every save.
log/


